Q: How to Programmatically Print the Windows Test Page for a Selected Printer in C# WinForms A: <p>I did a bit of digging, and Windows uses <code>printui.dll</code> to print the Test Page, so you should be able to use <code>Process.Start</code> to invoke the native Windows PrintUI command.</p>
<p>You can run it via `rundll32`</p>
<p>Here is the C# implementation:</p>
<pre><code>using System.Diagnostics;

// ...

string selectedPrinter = &quot;Your Printer Name&quot;;

Process.Start(&quot;rundll32.exe&quot;, string.Format(&quot;printui.dll,PrintUIEntry /k /n \&quot;{0}\&quot;&quot;, selectedPrinter));


</code></pre>

Q: Using the min() CSS function causes ancestors to increase in width A: <p>max-width does not change the <em>intrinsic width</em> of an image.</p>
<p>Your image is naturally <strong>800px</strong>, so even though it is visually scaled down to 400px, its ancestors still size themselves as if the image were 800px wide.</p>
<p>max-width: min(400px, 100%) only limits how large the image <em>may become</em>, but it does <strong>not</strong> change the width used for shrink-to-fit calculations.</p>
<pre><code>img {
  width: min(400px, 100%);
}
</code></pre>

Q: Why does my dotnet ef upgrade detect pending changes on the pipeline, but work locally? (.NET 10) A: <p>SO the snapshot comparison code did in fact work.</p>
<p>I disabled the warning, and deployed an endpoint to my dev server with a slightly extended version than the base from <a href="https://www.virtocommerce.org/t/pendingmodelchangeswarning-in-net-10-ef-core-10-what-happened-how-we-diagnosed-it/817" rel="nofollow noreferrer">the blog post</a></p>
<p>It turns out it all came from the following method</p>
<pre><code>    private static void SeedTable(EntityTypeBuilder&lt;EmailStandardFooterEntity&gt; builder)
    {
        builder.HasData(
            new EmailStandardFooterEntity
            {
                Id = Data.Enums.StandardFooterType.XemploStandard,
                FooterHtml = EmailFooterDataConstants.XemploFooter.StandardFooterHtml
            }
        );
    }
</code></pre>
<p>Where StandardFooterHtml is a const string built elsewhere. A multiline const string. When I ran migrations locally, it updated my snapshot with /r/n newlines in the footer data. When the container / pipeline checked for pending changes, it did so with /n newlines.</p>
<p>The fix was just <code>.Replace(&quot;\r\n&quot;, &quot;\n&quot;)</code> on the HasData.</p>
<p>The relevant code used to find out where my snapshot was messing up is:</p>
<pre><code>public static string GetPendingModelChanges(DbContext context)
{
    ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull(context);

    try
    {
        var serviceProvider = context.GetInfrastructure();
        var migrationsAssembly = serviceProvider.GetRequiredService&lt;IMigrationsAssembly&gt;();
        var migrationsModelDiffer = serviceProvider.GetRequiredService&lt;IMigrationsModelDiffer&gt;();
        var modelRuntimeInitializer = serviceProvider.GetRequiredService&lt;IModelRuntimeInitializer&gt;();

        var snapshotModel = migrationsAssembly.ModelSnapshot?.Model;
        if (snapshotModel == null)
        {
            throw new InvalidOperationException(&quot;No migrations exist yet - cannot get pending model changes.&quot;);
        }
        var initializedSnapshotModel = modelRuntimeInitializer.Initialize(snapshotModel, validationLogger: null);
        var snapshotRelationalModel = initializedSnapshotModel.GetRelationalModel();
        IModel currentModel;
        try
        {
            // Try to get design-time model (only available at design-time)
            var designTimeModelService = context.GetService&lt;IDesignTimeModel&gt;();
            currentModel = designTimeModelService?.Model ?? context.Model;
        }
        catch
        {
            // Fallback to runtime model if design-time model is not available
            currentModel = context.Model;
        }
        var currentRelationalModel = currentModel.GetRelationalModel();

        var differences = migrationsModelDiffer.GetDifferences(snapshotRelationalModel, currentRelationalModel);

        if (differences.Count == 0)
        {
            return $&quot;Found no pending model changes in {context.GetType().Name}&quot;;
        }

        var result = $&quot;Found {differences.Count} pending model change(s) in {context.GetType().Name}:\n&quot;;
        foreach (var difference in differences)
        {
            switch (difference)
            {
                case UpdateDataOperation update:
                    result += $&quot;  - UpdateDataOperation on table '{update.Table}':\n&quot;;
                    result += $&quot;      Keys: [{string.Join(&quot;, &quot;, update.KeyColumns)}]\n&quot;;
                    foreach(var change in update.KeyValues)
                    {
                        result += $&quot;      Key Values: [{string.Join(&quot;, &quot;, change)}]\n&quot;;
                    }
                    break;
                default:
                    result += $&quot;  - {difference}\n&quot;;
                    break;
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
    catch (Exception ex)
    {
        return $&quot;Error checking for pending model changes: {ex.Message}\nStack trace: {ex.StackTrace}&quot;;
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: How to avoid using import-csv for huge sized files? A: <p><code>.OpenText()</code> outputs a <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.io.streamreader" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>StreamReader</code></a> as you've already noted it, and the way to consume it is, <em>normally</em>, via <code>while</code> loop, so it isn't exactly comparable to <code>Get-Content</code> which outputs a stream of content:</p>
<pre><code>try {
    $path = Convert-Path big.txt
    $reader = [System.IO.File]::OpenText($path)
    while ($line = $reader.ReadLine()) {
        $line
    }
}
finally {
    if ($reader) {
        $reader.Dispose()
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>The .NET API that could be comparable to <code>Get-Content</code> but <strong>far more efficient</strong> since there is no wrapping of strings into <code>PSObject</code> and attachment of <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/scripting/developer/ets/properties" rel="nofollow noreferrer">ETS properties</a> would be <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.io.file.readlines" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>File.ReadLines()</code></a>, which in essence, is wrapping the <code>StreamReader</code> logic from above:</p>
<pre><code>$path = Convert-Path big.txt
# for improved performance use:
# `foreach ($line in [System.IO.File]::ReadLines($path)) { ... }`
[System.IO.File]::ReadLines($path) | ForEach-Object {
    $_
}
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>Alternatively, if you want to parse the CSV into objects, the <code>Import-Csv</code> parser (<a href="https://github.com/PowerShell/PowerShell/blob/4838a8d5f6c95339d4b44c8b402165e69e6ff929/src/Microsoft.PowerShell.Commands.Utility/commands/utility/CsvCommands.cs#L1235" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>CsvCommands.cs#L1235</code></a>) is actually very good (it can handle multi-line fields) and is efficient, its main performance problem is that it has to create dynamic objects (<code>PSObject</code>) which can make it pretty slow on large CSVs.</p>
<p>If you're on PowerShell 7+, I made an adaptation of their logic that can output an <code>IEnumerable&lt;T&gt;</code> using a schema class instead of dynamic objects, this lowers memory consumption and improves performance.</p>
<p>If you want to try it out:</p>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>$code = Invoke-RestMethod https://gist.githubusercontent.com/santisq/a3a105840baa783fea82b19515f55565/raw/2b21d21ab857035c3cb045e3a959f353e28ceb3a/ImportCsv.cs
Add-Type $code -IgnoreWarnings -WA 0
$path = Convert-Path big.csv

class MySchemaClass {
    # define a class that matches the headers in the CSV
    # see https://gist.github.com/santisq/a3a105840baa783fea82b19515f55565#file-usage-md for details
}

[ImportCsvHelper]::Read[MySchemaClass]($path)
</code></pre>
